Some formulation
======================

As ever, we aim to solve:

@f$ - \nabla ^2 u = - \partial_x^2 u - \partial_y^2 u = f @f$

which is a second-order equation. Let us first introduce a helper variable @f$ \phi @f$, so we can instead 
write in a first-order formulation:

@f$ \phi_x = \partial_x u, \ \phi_y = \partial_y u @f$.

This allows us to rewrite in first order, noting that the left hand side is just a divergence:

@f$ - \partial_x \phi_x - \partial_y \phi_y = f @f$ \n
@f$ - \text{div} \ \phi = f @f$.

## A note on the number of degrees of freedom

The DG formulation means that we have, for a given polynomial degree @f$ n @f$, and spatial dimension @f$ d@f$:

\li @f$ (n+1)^{d} @f$ degrees of freedom per cell
\li @f$ (n+1)^{d-1} @f$ degrees of freedom per face

We arrange these points spatially in a Gauss-Lobatto pattern.

We then aim to solve @f$ d+1 @f$ equations for each of these points:

\li We fix @f$ -\text{div} \phi = f @f$ at each of these points
\li We further fix  @f$ \partial_i u = \phi_i @f$ for each spatial dimension  @f$ i @f$.



## Let's integrate 

Since we have a first order formulation, we can simply integrate. We take the inner product with a test 
function @f$ \psi @f$, and then integrate over the cell:

@f$ \int_\text{cell} ( -\text{div} \phi, \psi ) dxdy = \int_\text{cell} ( f, \psi ) dxdy @f$.

The test function we typically use, at every point we have a degree of freedom, is the test function that is 1 at the spatial position of the degree of freedom, and 0 elsewhere.

Next, we want to just consider the left-hand integral. Let's integrate by parts:

\f{eqnarray*}{
      \int_\text{cell} ( -\text{div} \ \phi, \psi ) dxdy &=& \int_\text{cell} ( \phi, \nabla \psi ) dxdy - \int_\text{cell} \nabla ( \phi \psi ) dx dy \\ 

       &=& \int_\text{cell} ( \phi, \nabla \psi ) dxdy - \int_{\partial c} \phi \psi \cdot \hat{n} dS(x)
\f}

where in the second line we use Stokes' theorem to turn the second integral into an integral over the 
boundary of the cell (in other words over the face). We then make an approximation:

\f{eqnarray*}{
      &\approx& \int_\text{cell} ( \phi, \nabla \psi ) dxdy - \int_\text{face} [[ \phi \cdot \hat{n} ]] \cdot \psi dS(x)
\f}

Let's first handle the right-hand term, and explain what is meant by  @f$[[ \phi \cdot \hat{n} ]] @f$, hereafter referred to as @f$ \hat{\phi} @f$. 

## The right-hand term

The image below shows a simplistic, 1D diagram of a function @f$ u @f$, approximated in two cells by a 
piecewise linear function, marked in blue. 

@image html piecewise.png

We note that there is a discontinuity at the boundary for @f$ u @f$ and for its gradient, @f$ \phi @f$. 

So, as a first solution, we take an average, and define @f$\hat{\phi} = [[ \phi \cdot \hat{n} ]] @f$ to be the mean average of the gradient in the left-hand cell and the right-hand cell.

Let's now work out the contribution to the problem matrix that comes from this term. Let's now move to a 2D cell, where we have degree 1 Gauss-Lobatto points. This means we have 2 degrees of freedom on each face (which coincide spatially with the vertices), as well as 4 degrees of freedom on each cell (which again coincide with each vertex). This can be seen in page \ref multigrid_enumeration. Note that the vertices 
labelled (clockwise from bottom left) as 0, 2, 3, 1, which is an enumeration we keep throughout.

So, let's first evaluate the x component along face 0, which contains the vertices 0 and 2:

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \begin{pmatrix}
  1/2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
  0 & 0 & 1/2 & 0 \\
  \end{pmatrix}

  \cdot 

  \begin{pmatrix}
  \phi_x^0 \\
  \phi_x^1 \\
  \phi_x^2 \\
  \phi_x^3 \\
  \end{pmatrix}
\f}



