We note further that the @f$ \phi @f$ variable only packages up the gradients of @f$ u @f$ at certain points. We note that, for instance, @f$ \phi_x^0 = (u^1 - u^0)/h @f$, hence we can even rewrite the 
above equation as:

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \begin{pmatrix}
  -1/h & 1/h & 0 & 0 \\
  -1/h & 1/h & 0 & 0 \\
   0 & 0 & -1/h & 1/h  \\
  -1/h & 1/h & 0 & 0 \\

  \end{pmatrix}

  \cdot 

  \begin{pmatrix}
  1/2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
  0 & 0 & 1/2 & 0 \\
  \end{pmatrix}

  \cdot 

  \begin{pmatrix}
  \phi_x^0 \\
  \phi_x^1 \\
  \phi_x^2 \\
  \phi_x^3 \\
  \end{pmatrix}
\f}

