## The left-hand term

The first term tells us about the cell-cell coupling, and the second tells us about cell-face and face-cell. This is an integral over the whole cell, which we shall take as being 2D for this demo.
We look to update the components of @f$ \phi @f$ **should this be instead psi, the test function?**

Let's first note what the gradient along the @f$ x @f$ direction is along the bottom face. At the 
endpoint, we have the value @f$ u^1 @f$, and at the start we have @f$ u^0 @f$. Hence the gradient along
this face is simply @f$ (u^1 - u^0) / h @f$.

So then, to work out the values of @f$ \phi @f$ along the cell, we must linearly interpolate between
the value of the gradient on the bottom face and the value at the top face. Hence we evaluate:


\f{eqnarray*}{

  \int_\text{cell}
  \begin{pmatrix}
  (u^1 - u^0) / h \cdot y + (u^3 - u^2) / h \cdot (1 - y) \\

  \end{pmatrix}

  \cdot 

  \begin{pmatrix}
  (0 - 1) / h \cdot y + (0 - 0) / h \cdot (1 - y) \\

  \end{pmatrix}

  dy
\f}

Concluding remarks (this is how we think of things)
======================

\li Rewrite PDE in first-order formulation, with the helper @f$ \phi @f$.
\li Apply weak formulation over divergence PDE
\li Look at CC part
    - kick out the helpers (we can directly reconstruct them from our @f$ u @f$ values).
    - calculate entries 
\li Introduce another helper, @f$ \hat{\phi} @f$ to deal with discontinuity at boundary. We can use this to calculate contribution to Afc
\li Finally, set  up formula to compute @f$ \phi @f$ from @f$ u @f$


